 # Good — model knows exactly when and how
 "description": "Evaluate a mathematical expression. Use for arithmetic, trig, logarithms. Supports: +, -, *, /, **, sqrt, sin, cos, log, pi, e."
 ```
+
+For cancellation-sensitive metadata operations, `ctx.state.get(key, default=None,
+lock_deadline=deadline)` and `ctx.state.set(key, value, lock_deadline=deadline)` accept
+an optional absolute `time.monotonic()` deadline. The same deadline bounds the
+state registry guard, per-path thread lock, and native process lock; contention
+raises `TimeoutError` without a write. Pass one deadline through all reads and
+writes in a compound operation. Omitting it preserves blocking behavior. This
+bounds lock acquisition, not filesystem I/O, and keeps the native atomic writer,
+profile namespace, and quota checks.
+
+For compound operations containing separately bounded native work or filesystem
+I/O, pass one `LockAcquisitionBudget(seconds)` from `hermes_cli.plugins_state` as
+`lock_budget=budget` to every `get`/`set`. Only intervals spent acquiring the
+registry, per-path and process locks consume that shared allowance. The caller
+can include its own outer metadata lock using `with budget.acquiring() as deadline:` and acquire that lock with the remaining absolute deadline. End the
+acquisition scope as soon as the lock is acquired; protected work runs outside
+it. Exhaustion permanently refuses further acquisitions on that budget, even
+if locks are later free. Start one budget at the compound-operation boundary,
+never one per nested lock. The budget is sequential and thread-owned: copied
+contexts used on another thread or overlapping acquisition scopes fail closed
+without an additional lock or a fresh allowance. `lock_budget` and
+`lock_deadline` cannot be combined. The absolute-deadline option keeps its fixed
+wall-time cutoff; omitting both options keeps the original blocking behavior.
+Neither option extends a task grant or makes filesystem I/O preemptible.
